.TH "TEMPORAL.PLAINDATETIME.PLAINDATETIME" "3JS" "September 18, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Temporal.PlainDateTime.PlainDateTime \- Temporal.PlainDateTime()
constructor
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]Temporal.PlainDateTime()\f[B]\f[R] constructor creates
\f[CR]Temporal.PlainDateTime\f[R] objects.
.PP
This constructor allows you to create instances by directly supplying
the underlying data.
Like all other \f[CR]Temporal\f[R] classes, you should usually construct
\f[CR]Temporal.PlainDateTime\f[R] objects using the
\f[CR]Temporal.PlainDateTime.from()\f[R] static method, which can handle
a variety of input types.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
new Temporal.PlainDateTime(year, month, day)
new Temporal.PlainDateTime(year, month, day, hour)
new Temporal.PlainDateTime(year, month, day, hour, minute)
new Temporal.PlainDateTime(year, month, day, hour, minute, second)
new Temporal.PlainDateTime(year, month, day, hour, minute, second, millisecond)
new Temporal.PlainDateTime(year, month, day, hour, minute, second, millisecond, microsecond)
new Temporal.PlainDateTime(year, month, day, hour, minute, second, millisecond, microsecond, nanosecond)
new Temporal.PlainDateTime(year, month, day, hour, minute, second, millisecond, microsecond, nanosecond, calendar)
.EE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] \f[CR]Temporal.PlainDateTime()\f[R] can only be
constructed with \f[CR]new\f[R].
Attempting to call it without \f[CR]new\f[R] throws a
\f[CR]TypeError\f[R].
.RE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]year\f[R]
A number, truncated to an integer, representing the year in the ISO
calendar system.
.TP
\f[B]month\f[R]
A number, truncated to an integer, representing the month in the ISO
calendar system.
.TP
\f[B]day\f[R]
A number, truncated to an integer, representing the day of the month in
the ISO calendar system.
.TP
\f[B]hour\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A number, truncated to an integer, representing the hour component.
.TP
\f[B]minute\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A number, truncated to an integer, representing the minute component.
.TP
\f[B]second\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A number, truncated to an integer, representing the second component.
.TP
\f[B]millisecond\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A number, truncated to an integer, representing the millisecond
component.
.TP
\f[B]microsecond\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A number, truncated to an integer, representing the microsecond
component.
.TP
\f[B]nanosecond\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A number, truncated to an integer, representing the nanosecond
component.
.TP
\f[B]calendar\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A string representing the calendar to use.
See \f[CR]Intl.supportedValuesOf()\f[R] for a list of commonly supported
calendar types.
Defaults to \f[CR]\(dqiso8601\(dq\f[R].
Note that irrespective of the \f[CR]calendar\f[R], the \f[CR]year\f[R],
\f[CR]month\f[R], and \f[CR]day\f[R] must be in the ISO 8601 calendar
system.
.SS Return value
A new \f[CR]Temporal.PlainDateTime\f[R] object, representing the
date\-time specified by the parameters.
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]RangeError\f[R]
Thrown in one of the following cases:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Any date\-time component is not a finite number.
.IP \(bu 2
The date\-time component combination does not represent a valid date in
the ISO calendar system, or is not in the representable range, which is
±(10\(ha8 + 1) days, or about ±273,972.6 years, from the Unix epoch.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]calendar\f[R] is not a valid calendar identifier.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Using Temporal.PlainDateTime()
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] dt = \f[B]new\f[R] Temporal.PlainDateTime(2021, 7, 1);
console.log(dt.toString()); \f[I]// 2021\-07\-01T00:00:00\f[R]

\f[B]const\f[R] dt2 = \f[B]new\f[R] Temporal.PlainDateTime(2021, 7, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, \(dqhebrew\(dq);
console.log(dt2.toString()); \f[I]// 2021\-07\-01T00:00:00[u\-ca=hebrew]\f[R]
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Temporal.PlainDateTime\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Temporal.PlainDateTime.from()\f[R]
